// Policy 3 of 3 — "only refund an order you looked at first". A Dogwood policy.
//
// Cedar only sees the request in front of it. Dogwood, a superset of Cedar, can
// also look back at what already happened in this session. Here a refund is
// blocked unless the same customer opened that same order's transaction earlier
// in the session, so an agent can't refund an order nobody looked at.
//
// Like the $200 cap, this is a forbid layered on top of identity-binding.cedar:
// `unless temporal { … }` blocks when the thing inside did NOT happen.
//
// What it needs (LABS.md, M5):
//  - Created as a Dogwood policy: in the API the statement goes under `policy`,
//    not `cedar`.
//  - refund-gw-jwt's role allows bedrock-agentcore:GetWorkloadAccessToken
//    (policies/gateway_temporal_iam.json).
//  - Every call carries the x-amzn-bedrock-agentcore-policy-session-id header.
//    With a temporal policy attached, calls without it fail. The tester sends it.
//
// Only a *successful* lookup counts: a denied call is recorded as an error, not
// a response. Adding or changing a temporal policy ends open sessions (HTTP 409);
// the tester starts a new one by itself.

forbid (
    principal is AgentCore::OAuthUser,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"orders___process_refund",
    resource == AgentCore::Gateway::"YOUR_REFUND_GW_JWT_ARN"
)
unless temporal {
    formerly within 1h AgentCore::Action::"orders___get_order_transaction"::response{
        eventResource: resource,
        input.customer_id: context.input.customer_id,
        input.order_id: context.input.order_id
    }
};
